# ATOMIC-GATE-INPUTS: issue 92 — the preflight artifact and the provenance record

The last two non-atomic JSON writes the audit for #74 turned up. Both go through
`runlog.write_text_atomic`. The semantics match, so nothing else moves.

## The preflight artifact is a gate input, not a sentinel

That distinction is the whole reason it is worth a patch. A sentinel's failure mode is
permissive: `guard_dataset_write` reads `manifest.json`'s presence, so a truncated one wrongly
allows a later write. This artifact fails the other way. `require_preflight(consumer="training")`
opens it to decide whether a training run may start, and JSON that will not parse *raises* rather
than answering permissively.

So a truncated artifact causes no wrong decision. It causes **a refusal that looks like a failed
preflight**, at the start of the run the file exists to permit, with the cause invisible: the file
is there, and on a listing it is the right size. The next move a person makes is to re-run the
preflight, which loads the model.

That is also why the fixture asserts on the **previous** artifact surviving whole rather than
merely on the new one being absent. A re-run over a good artifact is the live case, and it is the
one where losing the file costs another model load to rebuild.

## The provenance record fails in a way its own contract does not cover

`runlog.git_commit` documents the rule this module lives by: provenance is a nice-to-have and must
never be the thing that kills a run. That covers failures in *collecting* provenance — no git, not
a repository, a timeout — and each answers "unknown". A failure in the **write** is different in
kind, and the contract says nothing about it: what survives parses as nothing and reads on a
listing as complete.

## The fixtures

Both use `_interrupt_the_manifest_writer` from `test_branch.py`, in the shape #74 established: it
truncates both writers, so the assertion rests on the outcome rather than on which writer is in
use. Both fail against the un-migrated code — checked by reverting each migration and running
them, not assumed.

## Left out, deliberately

The two `np.savez_compressed` writers in `probes/state_probe.py` at 1131 and 3198 use the same
temporary-and-replace shape and duplicate each other. They are binary and `runlog` has no bytes
variant. Giving it one, or de-duplicating those two, is a separate call and is named in the issue
rather than folded in here.

